🛡️ Defensive Takeaways (Portfolio Highlight)
| Issue                        | Why It Failed                  | Hardening Recommendation                  |

|-----------------------------|--------------------------------|-------------------------------------------|

| Fixed low exponent (e=5)  | Enables broadcast attack       | Use e=65537 or larger random coprime e |

| Same plaintext reused       | Perfect power recovery possible| Add random padding / nonce per request   |

| No OAEP/PKCS#1 padding     | Plaintext structure preserved  | Always use provably secure padding       |

| Custom RSA implementation   | Missed edge-case assumptions   | Use battle-tested libs (cryptography.io) |

Real-world detection: Rate-limit bulk encryption requests; monitor for scripted multi-key collection patterns.

📚 Learning Outcomes & Next Steps
Key lessons reinforced:

RSA is not “secure by large keys alone” — implementation details matter.

Low public exponents + repeated messages = instant win for attackers.

CRT + integer roots are powerful offensive math tools.

